@model Edulytics.Core.Curriculum.LessonContent.RichLessonV2

@if (Model.LessonCode == "PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G7:U03:L01")
{
  <section class="rich-lesson-section rich-lesson-section--g7-square-measurement"
           data-g7-u03-l01 aria-label="Grade 7 square measurement relationships">
    <h3>Measure, Graph, and Judge the Relationship — Edulytiks</h3>
    <figure>
      <img src="/curriculum/us-ccss-g7/geometry/u03-l01-square-measurement.svg"
           alt="Three squares with measured diagonals and exact perimeter and area values."
           loading="lazy" />
      <figcaption>Repeated measurements can miss an ideal line slightly without changing the mathematical relationship being investigated.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <h4>1.1 Collect square measurements</h4>
    <p>Measure or use supplied side and diagonal values for several squares.
       Record side length, diagonal length, perimeter, and area. Plot
       diagonal versus perimeter and diagonal versus area on separate graphs.</p>
    <h4>1.2 Decide which relationships are proportional</h4>
    <p>Explain why side length and perimeter are proportional. Use the
       measured diagonal data to decide whether diagonal and perimeter are
       consistent with a proportional relationship even if points do not
       land perfectly on one line. Contrast this with area.</p>
    <h4>1.3 Measurement error</h4>
    <p>Two students measure the same diagonal as 5.6 cm and 5.8 cm.
       Give realistic reasons for the difference and explain why a small
       measurement discrepancy is not enough by itself to reject proportionality.</p>
    <details><summary>Numerical self-check</summary>
      <p>Squares with sides 2, 4, and 6 have perimeters 8, 16, and 24
         and areas 4, 16, and 36. Perimeter scales directly with side,
         while area scales with the square of side length.</p>
    </details>
    <h4>Cool-down — first-party Edulytiks assessment</h4>
    <p>A class records square data with approximate diagonals 2.8, 5.7,
       and 8.5 for side lengths 2, 4, and 6. The matching perimeters are
       8, 16, and 24. Decide whether diagonal and perimeter are reasonably
       modeled as proportional, explain the role of measurement error,
       and state whether diagonal and area should be proportional.</p>
    <p><strong>Teacher review:</strong> Check the physical measurements,
       plotted graphs, explanation of scatter and distinction between
       experimental evidence and exact structure. Machine Practice checks
       only closed table, graph, and area subresults. The Cool-down is
       independently authored.</p>
  </section>
}

@if (Model.LessonCode == "PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G7:U03:L02")
{
  <section class="rich-lesson-section rich-lesson-section--g7-circle-features"
           data-g7-u03-l02 aria-label="Grade 7 defining features of a circle">
    <h3>What Makes a Shape a Circle? — Edulytiks</h3>
    <figure>
      <img src="/curriculum/us-ccss-g7/geometry/u03-l02-circle-features.svg"
           alt="A circle with center O, one radius, one diameter through the center, and the circumference labeled."
           loading="lazy" />
      <figcaption>A circle is the set of points at one fixed distance from its center; a diameter is two radii end to end through the center.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <h4>2.1 Sort and justify</h4>
    <p>Compare several round-looking shapes. Decide which are mathematical
       circles and justify each decision using equal distance from one center,
       not appearance alone.</p>
    <h4>2.2 Measure radius and diameter</h4>
    <p>Choose or draw a circle. Measure a radius in at least two directions
       and a diameter in at least two directions. Record ordinary measurement
       variation, then compare each diameter with twice the corresponding radius.</p>
    <h4>2.3 Construct a circle</h4>
    <p>Use a compass or another fixed-radius method to draw a circle with
       radius 4 cm. Mark the center, two radii, and one diameter. Explain why
       moving the pencil while keeping the fixed distance creates the circle.</p>
    <details><summary>Numerical self-check</summary>
      <p>If r=4 cm, d=8 cm. If d=11 cm, r=5.5 cm.
         Small differences in hand measurement do not change d=2r for an ideal circle.</p>
    </details>
    <h4>Cool-down — first-party Edulytiks assessment</h4>
    <p>Circle A has radius 3.5 cm and circle B has diameter 10 cm.
       Find the missing diameter and radius. Then state one defining
       property that both figures must satisfy to be circles and describe
       how you would check it with a measuring tool.</p>
    <p><strong>Teacher review:</strong> Check sorting justification, geometric
       construction, measurement technique and use of center/radius/diameter
       vocabulary. Machine Practice checks only exact radius-diameter
       conversion. The Cool-down is independently authored.</p>
  </section>
}
